Subject: [PATCH] crypt32: End base64 text of a multiple of 48 bytes with one separator.

CryptBinaryToString(CRYPT_STRING_BASE64, also with NOCR/NOCRLF) puts a separator after
every 64-character line and one after the last block. When the input is a multiple of
48 bytes the text ends with a full line, which then got the separator twice: once in
the loop and once after it. Windows ends the text with a single separator, and that is
also what the size query reserves room for:

 - CryptBinaryToStringA: the extra separator took the place of the terminating NUL;
   *pcchString was right, but the string was not terminated. Callers that use strlen()
   picked up whatever followed in memory.
 - CryptBinaryToStringW: lstrcpyW wrote the second separator and the NUL past the end
   of the caller's buffer.

Red Giant / Maxon's licence client (seen in Trapcode 3D Stroke; the other Red Giant
plugins talk to the same service) base64-encodes its encrypted request with
CryptBinaryToStringA and sends strlen() bytes over \\.\pipe\xfz_link. A 1152-byte
request (24 x 48) arrived as base64 with trailing garbage, decoded to 1153 bytes, the
Red Giant Service could not AES-decrypt it and never answered, and the plugin waited
forever: Media Encoder 2020 hung at "Loading 3DStroke.aex" on start-up.

Applied to the Proton-CachyOS 11.0 x86_64 crypt32.dll as a binary patch by
lib/patch_runner.py (lib/crypt32_caves.py). For the inlined A encoder the binary skips
the final separator instead of the in-loop one; the text is the same.
---
--- a/dlls/crypt32/base64.c	2026-10-02 18:44:37.557807471 +0300
+++ b/dlls/crypt32/base64.c	2026-10-02 18:44:37.571159068 +0300
@@ -126,7 +126,8 @@
         d += 3;
         div--;
 
-        if (i && i % 64 == 0)
+        /* the separator after the last full line is added below */
+        if (i && i % 64 == 0 && (div || pad_bytes))
             ptr += stradd(ptr, end, sep, strlen(sep));
     }
 
@@ -408,7 +409,8 @@
         d += 3;
         div--;
 
-        if (i && i % 64 == 0)
+        /* the separator after the last full line is added below */
+        if (i && i % 64 == 0 && (div || pad_bytes))
         {
             lstrcpyW(ptr, sep);
             ptr += lstrlenW(sep);
